    "answer": {
      "short": "$X\\times Y$ is countable.\n\nList the ordered pairs by zigzagging along the diagonals of a grid; every pair is reached after finitely many steps.",
      "worked": "If either set is empty, the product is empty and hence countable. If both sets are finite, their product is finite.\n\nOtherwise, list the elements without repetition as $X=\\{x_1,x_2,\\ldots\\}$ and $Y=\\{y_1,y_2,\\ldots\\}$, stopping a list if its set is finite. Place $(x_i,y_j)$ in column $i$, row $j$ of a grid.\n\nVisit successive diagonals $i+j=2,3,4,\\ldots$, alternating direction and omitting positions outside a finite list. The enumeration begins\n$$ (x_1,y_1),\\ (x_2,y_1),\\ (x_1,y_2),\\ (x_1,y_3),\\ (x_2,y_2),\\ (x_3,y_1),\\ldots $$\nEvery pair $(x_i,y_j)$ lies on diagonal $i+j$ and is reached after finitely many steps. No pair occurs twice. Thus the pairs can be put into a sequence indexed by $\\mathbb N$, so $X\\times Y$ is countable.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
